<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<Version>4.9.13-local.1</Version>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>v4.9.12: bundles forks 2.0.10. Generic-math f16 kernels: `NumericConvert.ToFloat32&lt;T&gt;`/`ToFloat64&lt;T&gt;` are transpilable generic converts (frontend convert-intrinsic, monomorphized to the per-type GPU cast) so a generic kernel can widen a generic numeric weight (`where TW : INumber&lt;TW&gt;`, TW = float|Half) to float/double for fp32/fp64 accumulation — the C# `float.CreateTruncating&lt;T&gt;` fails to lower (inspects typeof). ILGPU.Half now implements INumber&lt;Half&gt; / ISignedNumber&lt;Half&gt; (C# 11 generic math). A generic-math kernel (where T : INumber&lt;T&gt;) over Half now binds to Half's transpilable FP32 [MathIntrinsic] operators on all 6 backends, instead of being forced onto System.Half whose INumber members lower to a BitCast that fails codegen. Also fixes a latent bug: Half.One was new Half(0x1) == epsilon, not 1.0 == 0x3C00 — which made (Half)true produce epsilon in the bool-&gt;Half conversion (Convert.cs). Verified: generic-math Half kernel 9/9 backends + full sweep 3137 pass / 0 fail. v4.9.12-local.7 (local dev): perf — shader-RESOLUTION cache (WebGPUBackend.EnableShaderResolveCache, default ON) finishes eliminating per-dispatch O(WGSL) work. Caches the resolved compute shader per (compiled-kernel identity + dispatch-config signature: auto-grouped userDim, explicit GroupDim, dynamic-shared element count/size), so a re-dispatch of the same kernel at the same config skips GetOrCreateComputeShader and its O(WGSL-length) content HASH (the last O(WGSL) cost left after local.6). The key captures every resolution input — complete by construction (a hit returns the exact shader the resolve would produce); holds references to shaders the native accelerator's cache already owns (no GPU resources, no eviction). WebGPUAccelerator.ShaderResolveCacheHits/Misses telemetry + ClearShaderResolveCache(). Discrimination guard ShaderResolveCache_HitsAndDiscriminatesByDispatchSize (small-then-large auto-grouped dispatch catches a userDim-missing key). v4.9.12-local.6 (local dev): perf — eliminate per-dispatch O(WGSL) work in the WebGPU dispatch path (helps every high-dispatch-rate kernel: rendering loops, sims, fixed-shape ML decode). (1) WebGPUCompiledKernel.CompiledWorkgroupSize memoizes the @workgroup_size parsed ONCE from the WGSL, so the per-dispatch workgroup-patch compare and the dispatch-log record no longer run a full-WGSL regex (the regex now fires only on the rare KernelSpecialization-mismatch patch path). (2) The shader cache key is now a composite (wgsl, constants) tuple instead of a $"{wgsl}\n__CONSTANTS__:..." concatenation — no full-WGSL-length string allocation per dispatch. Behavior-preserving (identical workgroup-size parse; equivalent cache semantics). v4.9.12-local.5 (local dev): + per-dispatch CPU-PROLOGUE phase profiling under the same EnableDispatchProfiling flag — splits the .NET build/submit residual (the (forward − GPU-wait − readback) slice) into directly-measured phases: ProfileCpuShaderResolveMs (override-constant build + workgroup regex + shader lookup, O(WGSL)), ProfileCpuArgBuildMs (arg expand + reflection + scalar pack + bind-group entries + aliasing check), ProfileCpuBindGroupMs (CreateBindGroup + workgroup math), ProfileCpuEncodeMs (pass encode + dispatch-record), ProfileCpuDispatchCount. ResetDispatchProfiling zeros them. Measured (synthetic fixed-shape repeat): shaderResolve only ~4-8% (NOT the bottleneck), argBuild ~31-41%, bindGroup ~30%, encode ~26-30%. Additive, diagnostic, default OFF. v4.9.12-local.4 (local dev): + GPU-wait dispatch profiling now also times the GPU-&gt;CPU readback MapAsync(Read) surface (WebGPUBackend.ProfileReadbackWaitMs / ProfileReadbackWaitCount) - the OTHER place a step's GPU-wait hides (logits/shape readbacks queued behind prior work read ~0 in ProfileSyncWaitMs). Both surfaces now captured under the same EnableDispatchProfiling flag so a step's GPU-wait sums to the total instead of forcing a manual stitch of the consumer's readback timing. ResetDispatchProfiling zeros all four counters. Additive, diagnostic, default OFF - sync fields unchanged. v4.9.12-local.3 (local dev): + recur-only bind-group cache eviction - a (pipeline + buffer-bindings) signature is cached only on its 2nd sighting, so one-shot dispatches never park a live GPUBindGroup (bounds the cache; fixes the unbounded-growth / WebGPU framework-error mode at &lt;100% hit rate) + BindGroupCacheEntryCount telemetry. v4.9.12-local.2 (local dev): + gated GPU-wait dispatch profiling (WebGPUBackend.EnableDispatchProfiling / ProfileSyncWaitMs / ProfileSyncWaitCount / ResetDispatchProfiling) - splits a step's wall-time into GPU-wait (OnSubmittedWorkDone) vs .NET-side build to find where the per-step ML-decode floor actually goes. Diagnostic, default OFF. v4.9.12-local.1 (local dev): opt-in WebGPU bind-group cache - WebGPUBackend.EnableBindGroupCaching + WebGPUAccelerator.ClearBindGroupCache()/BindGroupCacheHits/BindGroupCacheMisses; reuses GPUBindGroups across identical-signature (fixed-shape decode) dispatches to cut the per-step GPU floor, keyed on buffer object identity. Default OFF. v4.9.11: Math.Clamp now works in kernels on all 6 backends (frontend remap to throw-free IntrinsicMath.Clamp); async browser parity (overridable SynchronizeAsync/CopyToCPUAsync core virtuals + real ReduceAsync drain), MemSetToZeroAsync, Wasm TensorView+Half struct-param dispatch, WebGL nested-struct hoisted init, opt-in Wasm host-buffer race detector. Bundles forks 2.0.8.</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
		<DebugType>Embedded</DebugType>
		<Title>SpawnDev.ILGPU</Title>
		<Authors>LostBeard</Authors>
		<Description>.Net GPU compute for the browser and desktop. Write once, run on WebGPU, WebGL, Wasm, CUDA, OpenCL, and CPU.</Description>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon-128.png</PackageIcon>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageTags>Blazor;BlazorWebAssembly;Cuda;OpenCL;WebGPU;WebGL;WebAssembly;Multithreading;WebWorkers;GPGPU;ILGPU;ParallelProcessing;ComputeShader;CrossPlatform</PackageTags>
		<NoPackageAnalysis>true</NoPackageAnalysis>
		<EnableDefaultCompileItems>true</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web" Version="10.0.2" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.BlazorJS" Version="3.5.12-local.1" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- =====================================================================
	     CRITICAL: FOUR-PACKAGE BUNDLE - read before bumping any one of these.
	     =====================================================================
	     A bug fixed under ILGPU/ ships in ILGPU.dll which lives in the SEPARATE
	     SpawnDev.ILGPU.Fork nupkg, NOT in this SpawnDev.ILGPU wrapper. If you
	     bump only this csproj, consumers transitively pull the OLD Fork from
	     nuget.org and the fix is invisible to them. To ship an ILGPU/ fix you
	     MUST bump all four together:

	       (1) ILGPU/ILGPU.csproj                                  <Version>
	       (2) ILGPU.Algorithms/ILGPU.Algorithms.csproj            <Version>
	       (3) SpawnDev.ILGPU/SpawnDev.ILGPU.csproj                <Version>
	       (4) The TWO PackageReference lines IMMEDIATELY BELOW
	           (the !Exists branch) - in-repo dev uses ProjectReference and
	           does not see these; the published nuspec dependency does.

	     Then run _check-fork-version-sync.bat at repo root to verify all four
	     match before commit. CI runs the same check on every push. If you skip
	     step (4) the consumer ships rc.N with stale Fork 2.0.x and the fix
	     never reaches the IR. This is exactly what bit rc.28 on 2026-04-28.
	     ===================================================================== -->
	<!-- Conditional fork reference: in-repo dev uses ProjectReference (fast iteration); external consumers use PackageReference via NuGet -->
	<ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\ILGPU\ILGPU.csproj')">
		<ProjectReference Include="..\ILGPU\ILGPU.csproj" />
		<ProjectReference Include="..\ILGPU.Algorithms\ILGPU.Algorithms.csproj" />
	</ItemGroup>
	<ItemGroup Condition="!Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\ILGPU\ILGPU.csproj')">
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU.Fork" Version="2.0.11-local.1" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU.Algorithms.Fork" Version="2.0.11-local.1" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="Assets\icon-128.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="wwwroot\icon-128.png" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<Content Remove="wwwroot\icon-128.png" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="windows" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="linux" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="macos" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
